 in written or spoken documents or presentations"}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn6le15ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Present Perfect Tense vs. The Simple Past Tense"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Present Perfect tense to talk about a finished action or event that is connected to the present in some way.<br><br>You use the Simple Past tense to talk about a finished action or event not necessarily connected to the present."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul><li>You can use the Present Perfect tense to talk about experience.</li><li>When you use the Present Perfect tense, you think about the past and present together. There is some connection between what happened in the past and now.</li><li><span class=\"italic\">I have learned English</span>= I learned English in the past and now I can speak English.</li><li><span class=\"italic\">He has bought a painting</span>= He bought the painting in the past and now he has a painting."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look at the chart about how to form the Present Perfect tense:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use have/has + the past participle of the verb in Present Perfect sentences."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Past Tense  \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Past Participle\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"love\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"loved\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"loved\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I've always loved jazz.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"become\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"became\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"become\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Montreal has become famous ...\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've always loved jazz. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Montreal has become famous for turning into a jazz haven every July."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Quantifiers"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use a quantifier before a noun to indicate the amount or number of an item. Quantifiers do not indicate a specific quantity but rather a general amount."}, {"type": "example", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul><li>You use quantifiers with both count and non-count nouns.</li> <li>Quantifiers like a <span class=\"italic\">few, several, many, dozens, hundreds, thousands</span>, or  <span class=\"italic\">millions</span>, can only be used with count nouns.</li> <li>You can use all of the quantifiers below with both count and non-count nouns.</li></ul>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Quantifier\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Meaning\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"more<br><br> less/fewer<br><br> no/none<br><br> any /not any<br><br> some<br><br> a lot of / lots of<br><br> plenty of<br><br> enough\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"a larger number/amount<br><br> a smaller number/amount<br><br> zero, nothing<br><br> to ask about / say there is not<br><br> a small number/amount<br><br> a very large number/amount<br><br> more than you need<br><br> as much as you need\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Many of the downtown streets were closed off to traffic. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Hundreds of musicians from dozens of countries perform in about 500 concerts."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "musicianship "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " the skills involved in performing music "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The musicianship between jazz performers is amazing."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob09aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to describe an outdoor music event."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "mesmerizing "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "holding someone's attention very intensely "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Jazz performers are mesmerizing."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob09aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to immerse oneself "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be completely involved in something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I immersed myself in the world's biggest jazz festival."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob09aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "closed off "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "isolated and secluded from something or someone "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Many of the downtown streets were closed off to traffic. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le15ob09aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to tune "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " to adjust to the correct setting or note "}, {"type": "part_speec